WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.751
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.751 --> 00:00:10.752
My ride back from work, on June 3rd,
2017, was not your typical commute.

00:00:10.752 --> 00:00:14.122
I was in the back seat
of a white pickup truck,

00:00:14.122 --> 00:00:18.405
with temperatures outside
edging up to 115 degrees.

00:00:18.405 --> 00:00:22.379
The roof of our car was painted
with a humanitarian symbol so

00:00:22.379 --> 00:00:25.171
that we wouldn't be hit by an airstrike.

00:00:25.171 --> 00:00:29.928
That summer, I was an aid worker
in Northern Iraq during the final

00:00:29.928 --> 00:00:33.404
stages of the operation
against Isis in Mosul.

00:00:33.404 --> 00:00:38.142
After a ten-hour day in the fields,
covered in dust,

00:00:38.142 --> 00:00:42.474
all I wanted was to get home and
take a cold shower.

00:00:42.474 --> 00:00:44.555
But the day wasn't over yet.

00:00:44.555 --> 00:00:49.134
As my colleagues and I made our way home,
we started hearing reports that

00:00:49.134 --> 00:00:52.824
neighborhoods in Mosul were
filling with poisonous gas.

00:00:52.824 --> 00:00:56.329
Twitter was lighting up with
these cell phone images showing

00:00:56.329 --> 00:00:59.430
aircraft firing white sprays
of smoke into the city.

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:04.169
It was quickly identified
as white phosphorus used by

00:01:04.169 --> 00:01:07.968
the US-led coalition and the Iraqi army.

00:01:07.968 --> 00:01:11.692
White phosphorus can be
used legitimately or

00:01:11.692 --> 00:01:16.206
more as a smoke screen to
obscure military movements.

00:01:16.206 --> 00:01:22.867
But when humans come into contact with it,
it can also burn flesh off of the bone.

00:01:22.867 --> 00:01:25.494
So depending on how it's used,

00:01:25.494 --> 00:01:30.761
it can either be a totally legitimate
tactic of war or war crime.

00:01:30.761 --> 00:01:35.694
And sometimes it's nearly
impossible to tell the difference.

00:01:35.694 --> 00:01:38.080
My colleagues began to argue.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:41.657
Should we demand
an international outcry for

00:01:41.657 --> 00:01:46.558
the coalitions use of chemicals
in a densely populated city?

00:01:46.558 --> 00:01:52.240
Or given that it’s a grey area,
should we focus energy on other problems?

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:55.135
It was a judgment call.

00:01:55.135 --> 00:01:59.605
A decision made by a handful
of well meaning humanitarians.

00:01:59.605 --> 00:02:03.807
We decided not to push it.

00:02:03.807 --> 00:02:07.019
I was left confused by the whole incident.

00:02:07.019 --> 00:02:11.644
How could something as weighty
as a war crime be so slippery?

00:02:11.644 --> 00:02:14.615
So hard to pin down.

00:02:14.615 --> 00:02:19.527
Back in the States, I began studying
war crimes, and I quickly learnt

00:02:19.527 --> 00:02:24.521
that there's no clear answer, and
the technology of war is evolving so

00:02:24.521 --> 00:02:28.370
quickly, the question is
only becoming more urgent.

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:33.983
The concept of war crimes originates
in the earliest eras of human history.

00:02:33.983 --> 00:02:40.688
Ancient codes of war from China, India and
Greece, regulated troops conduct.

00:02:40.688 --> 00:02:45.492
Today, the laws have all provide
guidance on who qualifies as combatants,

00:02:45.492 --> 00:02:50.009
the limits of the battlefields and
what sorts of acts are permissible.

00:02:50.009 --> 00:02:54.825
Troops must wear uniforms,
they should avoid civilians,

00:02:54.825 --> 00:02:59.077
they can't torture prisoners,
things like that.

00:02:59.077 --> 00:03:01.845
When you think about it,
it's actually kind of bizarre.

00:03:01.845 --> 00:03:07.560
Acts that would ordinarily land you in
jail, like taking someone's life or

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:12.218
destroying a building,
are totally legal if you're at war.

00:03:12.218 --> 00:03:16.974
But even in war, there is
a category of actions that humanity

00:03:16.974 --> 00:03:21.836
had decided is beyond the pale,
and those we call war crimes.

00:03:21.836 --> 00:03:27.231
We even have tribunals that attempt
to being perpetrators to justice.

00:03:27.231 --> 00:03:33.057
Like following the Rwandan genocide or
ethnic cleansing in the Balkans.

00:03:33.057 --> 00:03:37.599
To me,
there's something profound about humans

00:03:37.599 --> 00:03:42.690
attempting to being integrity and
ethics into battle.

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:46.489
But in practice, it's not so
easy to draw the line.

00:03:46.489 --> 00:03:50.396
Partially because world's
feeling messy and chaotic, but

00:03:50.396 --> 00:03:55.538
also because it turns out there is no
clear universal definition of a war crime.

00:03:55.538 --> 00:04:00.936
Ask any international lawyer and
you'll get this circular answer,

00:04:00.936 --> 00:04:05.040
a war crime is a serious
violation of the laws of war.

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:08.236
What's a serious violation
the laws of war?

00:04:08.236 --> 00:04:10.672
A war crime.

00:04:10.672 --> 00:04:15.805
As a result, historically courts
have sometimes struggled to decide,

00:04:15.805 --> 00:04:17.663
is terrorism a war crime?

00:04:17.663 --> 00:04:19.245
Is rape?

00:04:19.245 --> 00:04:23.033
Is recruiting child soldiers?

00:04:23.033 --> 00:04:28.120
If you think it's confusing now,
it's about to get a whole lot worse.

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:31.838
Technology being developed
right here in Silicon Valley,

00:04:31.838 --> 00:04:35.704
artificial intelligence,
autonomous vehicles, drones,

00:04:35.704 --> 00:04:39.443
is the same technology that
will dictate the future of war.

00:04:39.443 --> 00:04:42.600
And it's evolving so rapidly,
it's already outpaced the law.

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:47.522
We're headed for a legal black hole,
where anything goes.

00:04:47.522 --> 00:04:52.098
In particular,
technology is complicating the three

00:04:52.098 --> 00:04:55.587
areas where law used to provide clarity.

00:04:55.587 --> 00:05:01.661
Who are the combatants, where is
the battlefield, and what are the harms?

00:05:01.661 --> 00:05:05.894
First, who are the commandants?

00:05:05.894 --> 00:05:09.595
Picture a fully autonomous
AI power weapon system.

00:05:09.595 --> 00:05:13.346
To be clear, this doesn't exist yet,
but it's not inconceivable.

00:05:13.346 --> 00:05:19.228
It's been pre-programmed to strike enemy
targets without human intervention,

00:05:19.228 --> 00:05:24.114
it may actually be more precise and
accurate, less prompt to error.

00:05:24.114 --> 00:05:25.660
But it's imperfect.

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:29.639
And let's say it somehow
commits a horrendous act like

00:05:29.639 --> 00:05:33.283
targeting a hospital
instead of a military base.

00:05:33.283 --> 00:05:35.897
Who can be held responsible?

00:05:35.897 --> 00:05:39.794
Technology creates a massive
accountability gap.

00:05:39.794 --> 00:05:43.027
War crimes are committed by combatants but

00:05:43.027 --> 00:05:47.350
are computer programmers and
manufacturers commands?

00:05:47.350 --> 00:05:51.948
Unless it could be proven that they
were somehow aware of the defect,

00:05:51.948 --> 00:05:55.132
they're simply too far
remove from the crime.

00:05:55.132 --> 00:05:59.886
And would it even be fair to
charge an engineer in Palo Alto

00:05:59.886 --> 00:06:04.257
with war crimes committed
halfway along the globe.

00:06:04.257 --> 00:06:10.049
Second, where is the battlefield?

00:06:10.049 --> 00:06:14.818
Imagine a nuclear explosion in space,
creates an electromagnetic pulse that

00:06:14.818 --> 00:06:19.163
reaches Earth's atmosphere,
causing aircraft to fall from the sky.

00:06:19.163 --> 00:06:23.110
Or imagine a cyber attack launched
from a suburban living room.

00:06:23.110 --> 00:06:25.981
That wipes out a power
grid in another country.

00:06:25.981 --> 00:06:30.295
The law of work tries to keep
destruction on the battlefields, but

00:06:30.295 --> 00:06:34.302
there's no standalone regulation
of warfare and space, and

00:06:34.302 --> 00:06:37.550
there's no consensus on
the limits of cyber war.

00:06:37.550 --> 00:06:43.893
Wars of the future will rarely look like
two armies meeting on the battlefields,

00:06:43.893 --> 00:06:47.532
where we'll beef up from
remote locations and

00:06:47.532 --> 00:06:52.214
it will be carried out in outer spance and
in digital space.

00:06:52.214 --> 00:06:55.816
And third, what are the harms?

00:06:55.816 --> 00:07:01.156
The law of work usually conceives
a harm in terms of physical damage,

00:07:01.156 --> 00:07:04.298
violence, bloodshed, destruction.

00:07:04.298 --> 00:07:10.070
But new weapons will cause different
harms, like environmental damage.

00:07:10.070 --> 00:07:14.975
Imagine a weapon leaves behind toxic
remnants that slowly destroys farm land,

00:07:14.975 --> 00:07:16.666
leaving it inhospitable.

00:07:16.666 --> 00:07:21.453
Or imagine a cyber attack
that destroys information,

00:07:21.453 --> 00:07:24.686
medical records, financial data.

00:07:24.686 --> 00:07:30.190
We haven't yet decided if those sorts
of harms should be off limit and were.

00:07:30.190 --> 00:07:36.885
There's a work crime require
physical pain, physical destruction.

00:07:36.885 --> 00:07:39.249
These questions are not easily answered.

00:07:39.249 --> 00:07:43.420
The law of work was created before
artificial intelligence even before

00:07:43.420 --> 00:07:44.334
the Internet.

00:07:44.334 --> 00:07:48.598
We're retrofitting rules from the 19th and
20th century to a new world.

00:07:48.598 --> 00:07:52.785
And frankly, these are questions
that no one wants to think about.

00:07:52.785 --> 00:07:57.877
The latest version of the United States
official guidelines on autonomous

00:07:57.877 --> 00:08:03.143
weapons is no more than a couple page
PDF document with track changes left on.

00:08:03.143 --> 00:08:07.037
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That's terrifying.

00:08:07.037 --> 00:08:11.801
Some say it's not worth establishing legal
guidelines if you're not sure that we can

00:08:11.801 --> 00:08:13.731
hold people accountable anyway.

00:08:13.731 --> 00:08:17.690
Some say that regulation
will stifle innovation.

00:08:17.690 --> 00:08:22.922
I say, it's a cop out,
I don't have all the answers.

00:08:22.922 --> 00:08:28.545
But my instinct is that war is and
should remain a uniquely human endeavor.

00:08:28.545 --> 00:08:32.897
There should be meaningful human
control over life and death decisions.

00:08:32.897 --> 00:08:37.298
For sure, technology can do
a lot to improve defense and

00:08:37.298 --> 00:08:41.145
reduce risk for
service members and civilians.

00:08:41.145 --> 00:08:45.398
But we can never fully delegate
moral agency to a machine.

00:08:45.398 --> 00:08:48.141
And we should resist
the temptation to try.

00:08:48.141 --> 00:08:54.738
At Stanford, we're tasked with
thinking up the unimaginable.

00:08:54.738 --> 00:08:58.441
A new business idea,
the moon shot invention.

00:08:58.441 --> 00:09:03.376
The sobering side of that mandate is
the duty to also consider the harms.

00:09:03.376 --> 00:09:07.607
What the adverse actor might
do if given a chance or

00:09:07.607 --> 00:09:12.454
the well meaning actor who
simply has no legal guidance.

00:09:12.454 --> 00:09:14.744
This isn't limited to welfare,

00:09:14.744 --> 00:09:20.479
ethic should be a central component of all
technology design not an after thought.

00:09:20.479 --> 00:09:24.988
The mere act of writing down standards
helps to build the consensus on

00:09:24.988 --> 00:09:27.643
what's acceptable, and what isn't.

00:09:27.643 --> 00:09:32.095
We can't trust that existing
law has all of the answers,

00:09:32.095 --> 00:09:36.095
as is clear with war crimes,
but may have very few.

00:09:36.095 --> 00:09:41.512
War is a tragic reality, but
the decisions we make today can ensure

00:09:41.512 --> 00:09:47.622
that it is constrained to the battlefield
and the soldiers who fight on it.

00:09:47.622 --> 00:09:48.592
Thank you.

00:09:48.592 --> 00:09:54.641
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:54.641 --> 00:09:56.050
[MUSIC]

